Day 22 — Function pointers and command-line arguments

Learn: Function pointers, callbacks, passing functions as arguments, argc, argv, program arguments.

Practice:
- Make a calculator using function pointers.
- Pass an array and its size to a function.
- Write a program that adds two numbers supplied through command-line arguments.
- Build a menu using function pointers.



23

Day 23 — Linked lists

Learn: Self-referential structures, nodes, malloc(), traversal, insertion, deletion.

Practice:
- Create a singly linked list.
- Insert a node at the beginning and end.
- Delete a node by value.
- Search for a value.
- Reverse a linked list.



24

Day 24 — Stack and queue

Learn: LIFO, FIFO, array-based stack, queue, circular queue, overflow and underflow.

Practice:
- Implement stack push, pop, and peek.
- Implement queue enqueue and dequeue.
- Build a circular queue.
- Reverse a string using a stack.



25

Day 25 — Advanced data structures and algorithms

Learn: Binary trees, tree traversals, binary search trees, time complexity, space complexity, Big-O notation.

Practice:
- Build a simple binary tree.
- Implement inorder, preorder, and postorder traversal.
- Insert values into a binary search tree.
- Search for and count nodes in the tree.
- Compare linear search and binary search complexity.



26

Day 26 — Debugging, errors and safe C

Learn: Syntax errors, logical errors, runtime errors, compiler warnings, debugging, assertions, undefined behavior, bounds checking.

Practice:
- Find and fix 10 intentionally broken programs.
- Identify an array out-of-bounds error.
- Fix a null-pointer bug.
- Fix a memory leak.
- Compile with warnings enabled, for example gcc -Wall -Wextra -g main.c -o main.
Learn to test edge cases such as zero, negative numbers, empty strings, and maximum input sizes.



27

Day 27 — Advanced C concepts

Learn: Storage duration and linkage, volatile, const, restrict, typedef, type qualifiers, integer overflow, implementation-defined behavior, portability.

Practice:
- Write a generic-looking swap function using separate functions for different types.
- Use qsort() with a comparison function.
- Create a lookup table using an array of function pointers.
- Explore how sizeof behaves with arrays and pointers.
- Research why volatile matters for memory-mapped hardware registers.



28

Day 28 — Project 1: Student management system

Build a command-line application with:
- Add, view, search, update, and delete student records.
- Store name, roll number, and marks using structures.
- Save and load records from a file.
- Use functions to organize the program.
- Validate input and handle file errors.
Challenge: Make the data persist after the program closes.



29

Day 29 — Project 2: Inventory or banking system

Build a menu-driven application with:
- Account creation or product registration.
- Deposit and withdrawal, or stock additions and sales.
- Search and update records.
- File-based data persistence.
- Input validation and transaction history.
Use structures, arrays or linked lists, functions, and file handling.

Challenge: Separate the code into multiple .c and .h files.



30

Day 30 — Final boss challenge

Create a Library Management System in C from scratch.

Requirements:
- Add, display, search, update, and delete books.
- Issue and return books.
- Store records in files.
- Use structures, pointers, and dynamic memory where appropriate.
- Organize the code using functions and separate files.
- Validate inputs and handle errors.
- Test the program with at least 10 different cases